    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The fabric is definitely high-quality 100% poly, and we've swapped papers too, but it's still happening. It's almost weird—it feels less like a chemical failure and more like the color is just being... taken. Like it's intentionally being pulled away or prevented from settling in certain spots. Is there any way environmental factors or even some kind of interference could make the pigment behave that erratically?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Erratic pigment behavior, especially if it feels like the color is being \"pulled away,\" suggests we need to look beyond just the press settings. Environmental factors can play a role, such as humidity in your pressing area, which can affect how the ink transfers. Another possibility, though less common, is that the polyester itself has a surface coating or sizing agent that is interfering with the dye uptake. You might want to pre-treat the fabric with a specialized fixative spray, if your supplier recommends one for that specific blend.",
      "source": "target",
